# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# kenzed.in — static Next.js export
#
# Served by OpenLiteSpeed, which implements Apache's .htaccess syntax but is
# NOT Apache. Two consequences drive the whole shape of this file:
#
#  1. `<IfModule>` blocks are evaluated against modules LiteSpeed actually
#     reports. It reports mod_rewrite (it has its own rewrite engine) but NOT
#     mod_dir, mod_headers, mod_deflate or mod_mime — so anything wrapped in
#     those guards is silently skipped. Wrapping `DirectorySlash Off` in
#     `<IfModule mod_dir.c>` meant it never applied, and every `Header set`
#     inside `<IfModule mod_headers.c>` meant the site shipped with no
#     security headers and no asset caching at all. Directives that LiteSpeed
#     supports natively are therefore declared bare, unguarded.
#
#  2. Next 16 emits an RSC-payload DIRECTORY next to each page file
#     (`live-projects/` alongside `live-projects.html`). LiteSpeed's built-in
#     directory handler wants to 301 `/live-projects` to `/live-projects/`,
#     while the rewrite below wants to serve `live-projects.html`. Those two
#     race per request: the observed result was `/live-projects` ->
#     `/live-projects/` -> `/live-projects` -> ..., an intermittent infinite
#     redirect loop on every page of the site. The rewrite is ordered ahead of
#     any slash handling and terminated with [END] rather than [L] so the
#     ruleset is never re-entered, and BOTH URL forms resolve straight to the
#     exported page instead of one bouncing to the other.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

Options -MultiViews -Indexes
DirectoryIndex index.html

# Bare, not inside <IfModule mod_dir.c> — see note 1.
DirectorySlash Off

RewriteEngine On

# Canonical host: strip www.
RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^www\.(.+)$ [NC]
RewriteRule ^ https://%1%{REQUEST_URI} [L,R=301]

# Build assets are served verbatim.
RewriteRule ^_next/ - [END]

# An exact file on disk wins (index.html, favicon.ico, sitemap.xml, ...).
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} -f
RewriteRule ^ - [END]

# Clean URL -> exported page. This MUST come before any directory handling,
# and [END] is load-bearing: [L] only ends the current pass, and the directory
# handler gets another chance on the next one.
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME}.html -f
RewriteRule ^(.*)$ /$1.html [END]

# Trailing slash -> canonical bare URL, one hop. This is safe now only because
# the rule above terminates the bare form with [END]: the loop existed because
# the bare form was itself being 301'd back to the slash form by the directory
# handler, so the two redirects chased each other. `%{DOCUMENT_ROOT}/$1.html`
# was tried here instead and does not resolve under LiteSpeed — it sent every
# slash URL to the 404 page — so the redirect is the correct mechanism.
# KNOWN LIMITATION, deliberately left as-is.
# `/about/` returns 404. This rule does not fire and cannot: with
# DirectorySlash Off, LiteSpeed resolves the request to the RSC payload
# directory, finds no index.html and returns 404 WITHOUT ever consulting the
# document-root rewrite rules. Both rule-pattern (`^(.+)/$`) and REQUEST_URI
# (`^/(.+)/$`) forms were tested; neither is reached.
#
# Moving the redirect to a context-scope `rewrite { rules }` in the vhost DOES
# reach it, but doing so stops the .htaccess rules applying to that context —
# the .html rewrite then loses its race with the directory handler and the
# original infinite loop returns. That was tried and rolled back.
#
# Nothing on the site links to the slash form: every href, the sitemap and every
# canonical tag use the bare URL. The durable fix is `trailingSlash: true` in
# next.config.ts, which makes Next emit `about/index.html` so there is no
# name collision at all — but it changes every public URL to `/about/`, which
# is an SEO decision rather than a bug fix.
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI} ^/(.+)/$
RewriteRule ^ /%1 [R=301,END]

ErrorDocument 404 /404.html

# ---------- response headers ----------
# NOT set here. `Header` is a mod_headers directive and OpenLiteSpeed does not
# implement it in .htaccess at all — guarded or bare, it is ignored, which is
# why this site shipped with no security headers and no asset caching. They are
# declared in the vhost's `extraHeaders` block instead
# (/usr/local/lsws/conf/vhosts/kenzed.in/vhost.conf), which is the only place
# LiteSpeed honours them. Keep the two in step: if this file moves to a real
# Apache host, the headers have to come back here.

# ---------- mime ----------
AddType application/javascript .js
AddType image/svg+xml .svg
AddType font/woff2 .woff2
